00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
 
on august 24  2020 as covid19 cases were spiking  across the state  the university of illinois welcomed  students back to campus at  urbana-champaign for the fall semester  the decision was informed by months of  planning and research  according to an epidemiological model  developed by university professors  there could be a relatively safe way to  open campus for in-person classes  you're doing 10 000 to 15 000 tests a  day  we're testing everybody on our campus at  illinois urbana champaign  twice a week results can be accessed on  an app  things didn't go according to plan on  september 1st just a week after students  returned to campus  there was an outbreak two weeks in the  university of illinois reported more  than 700 cases which is more than they  expected for the semester  and the university of illinois wasn't  alone at this point we should not be  surprised  that the model got it wrong it sounds  like i'm saying i don't really trust the  experts  because i don't why does it seem like  covet 19 models are getting it so  wrong in reality there's a lot more  going on here  in order to understand how models can be  useful we first have to understand how  they're built  what they can and can't tell us and how  all of this  often gets lost in translation  epidemiology  is not rocket science it's harder  when scientists build an epidemiological  model what they're really doing is  writing down a recipe  for how they think the reality of an  epidemic unfolds  and adding math so that they can project  that reality into different possible  futures  typically in a model what you'll do is  first write down  what we call the variables these are the  things that are changing over time  and then in the next steps what you're  going to write down is just how those  different variables  interact and then what you hope to do is  take  some observations about the real world  and help to  parameterize your model how fast does  the disease spread from one person to  another  how many contacts does a typical person  of some  age have with people of other ages  scientists use two main approaches to  building models of covid19  agent-based and compartmental models  most models are a combination of both  with an agent-based model our goal is to  think about individual agents  in this case people and we give them  certain rules or certain behaviors  and then can simulate forward based on  those behaviors how they interact  and in the case of covet how they spread  disease and how their behaviors might  change  because of the disease around them but  agent-based models don't easily scale to  very  large populations they can also require  a prohibitive amount of computational  resources to run  so for a broader perspective scientists  also use compartmental models  or seir models everyone in the  population  falls into one of these categories s  for susceptible to the disease e for  exposed  i for infectious and r for recovered or  removed  you can think about these compartments  as different stages of the disease  modelers use differential equations to  simulate how people move from one stage  to the next  based on data like infection rate  hospitalization rate  and fatality rate scientists can use  approaches like compartmental and  agent-based models to approximate  reality  but reality is a complicated and  unpredictable place  rife with uncertainty first off we all  go in assuming we're going to be wrong  we understand that there is no way to  get it  right there is no such thing as a model  that will  exactly represent the world or can  possibly  capture every one of the dynamics in the  real world  the prediction is very sensitive to data  to how good data is  data is dispersed is incomplete  and is not taken with  one uniform protocol everywhere when you  don't have good data  you cannot have good predictions small  differences in the parameter are  amplified as the dynamics of the system  progresses  so this is very similar to the butterfly  effect  a slightly different initial condition  leads to a very different  outcome this exponential sensitivity  causes differences to amplify  exponentially fast so you have a  spreading cone  of possible trajectories of your future  but there's an even deeper source of  uncertainty that lurks in every model  that source is us  when we look at human behavior and human  dynamics there's a huge amount of  uncertainty there  as you can imagine our daily mobility  patterns our daily contact patterns  are so out of the norm we've been  scrambling  to find data to get  insight into how people are behaving  during covet and how that behavior is  changing on  you know a weekly basis in the absence  of data on relevant human behaviors  modelers have to make assumptions a  small error or a missed assumption can  have profound consequences on the  model's outcome  and even correct assumptions can change  over time  causing the model to veer way off course  from its original projections  this is why nobody can predict the  future  not even modelers models  fundamentally depend on what are the  assumptions that you make  we made assumptions that people would be  partying we estimated  how much that would be going on  what we didn't take into account is we  didn't expect that people would be  non-compliant  in other words if you're told to isolate  we assume that people would actually  do that and that assumption was one of  the most  erroneous ones that we made  [Music]  as a modeler you have to try to focus on  one of the important things  and they may not be the things that you  would spend most of your time  on as a scientist writing paper for  publication but if you're doing that but  meanwhile  you've left out the fact that a third of  the people in your population  don't even believe that you should be  even trying to  mitigate coverage that's a far more  important  source of error the statistician george  e p  box once famously said all models are  wrong  but some are useful instead of  predictions  something modelers can offer us is  projections  these are glimpses into a range of  possible futures that we have the power  to change  the best early models are the ones that  are wrong  precisely because we react to them and  make them wrong so  when we see projections about what could  happen were we to do nothing at all  the fact that those projections are  invariably wrong  is fantastic because that means we  actually respond to the model's  predictions and  prove them wrong but even when a model  does the job it's supposed to do  there's a risk that it could be  misunderstood when the worst case  scenario  doesn't come to pass how do we  communicate  when we can't predict the future but we  can provide scenario predictions right  so  and this this is a real challenge for um  for scientists for modelers but it's  also a really important responsibility  we have  early in the pandemic one of the very  first requests we received from  policymakers trying to understand what  the threat was and trying to  figure out what the right path forward  was was a request from the white house  coronavirus  task force what do your models say about  how many people are going to die from  coveted 19 in 2020  we responded and said you know we don't  have a crystal ball it depends on the  policies it depends on people's behavior  so we we can't give you a forecast but  we can make some projections  under a number of different scenarios  for example we can make projections  under the assumption  that we do not do anything to slow the  spread of this virus  or we'll consider sort of the opposite  extreme we'll assume that we're staying  strict for the foreseeable future  and then let's consider something in  between where we're sort of on a roller  coaster of trying to contain this virus  and the forecasts under those scenarios  look very different there's been a lot  of what they call  modeling in the science of infectious  diseases that's been done  that is now being understood to have  been really wrong  there's a lot of misunderstanding about  the application of models  but i'd love to see the public take away  from our modeling for covin  is that this is one tool in the toolkit  it's really important for us all to  understand how the tools get used  nobody knew anything about  epidemiological modeling  12 months ago all of a sudden we're  seeing the results of modeling on the  nightly news  and i find it fascinating and and and  really  pretty awe inspiring that there's this  huge uptick in  really scientific awareness and  awareness of what mathematical modeling  even  is and i think a new appreciation for  the fact that  it's out there so these pandemics will  come again we are  a relationship with nature that favors  that the pathogens jump from one species  to another  so for the future what i envision is  something similar to what  we currently do with weather forecasts  we have full institutions we have a  surveying systems with many  weather stations all over our  territories that inform us of the  current conditions everywhere  and this is fed into models that  continuously update their forecasts why  don't we have that for pandemics  this is not a matter of a single country  we have to be together  in this so when we face a new uh  challenge and they will come then we are  ready just to  plug in the new data that instantly  arrives right and say something about  how this can spread  [Music]  [Music]  you

